{"message":"The pushback I'm going to get is: "You're privileging an abstract consent ideal over employees and customers who depend on the free product. If we miss the revenue target, 20-30 people may lose jobs. Users can still say no. You're making the perfect the enemy of the good."

If I propose a compromise: keep equal buttons, no social proof, but default the toggle on in the detailed controls and use "Best experience" / "Basic experience" labels instead of Allow / Don't allow. That probably recovers half the gap.

Would you accept that compromise given the stakes, or is default-on for data sharing also a hard no?"}

